<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Histri</h1><p class="st-meta">600 BCE – 177 BCE (approx.) · capital Nesactium</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Iron Age people of the Istrian peninsula, centred on Nesactium, whose resistance to Rome ended with the fall of their stronghold in 177 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Histri, who gave their name to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/histria">Istria</a>, emerged from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> hillfort culture of the peninsula in the early first millennium BCE. Rich graves at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nesactium">Nesactium</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pula">Pula</a> show a wealthy elite with links to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ancient-veneti">Veneti</a> and the Adriatic by the 6th century BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A king ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nesactium">Nesactium</a> over hillforts (castellieri) scattered across the peninsula. The Histri farmed, kept herds and were notorious to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> preying on Adriatic shipping. Stone sculpture at Nesactium, including figures of riders and mothers, shows a distinctive local art influenced by Italian and Greek styles.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Roman campaigns began in 221 BCE after attacks on grain ships. After the founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a> in 181 BCE war resumed, and in 177 BCE the consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-claudius-pulcher-consul-177-bce">Gaius Claudius Pulcher</a> stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nesactium">Nesactium</a>; King Epulo killed himself. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/histria">Istria</a> was later joined to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> under Augustus.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 600 BCE – 177 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/